---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnixTimestampConverter from '@/components/tools/datetime/UnixTimestampConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="unix-timestamp-converter">
  <UnixTimestampConverter client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What a Unix timestamp is</h2>
    <p>
      A Unix timestamp, also called epoch time or POSIX time, is the number of seconds since 1 January 1970 at
      00:00:00 UTC, not counting leap seconds. It is a single integer with no time zone, calendar or formatting,
      so databases, log files, JWTs, HTTP caches and APIs use it. Two systems on opposite sides of the world
      read the same value the same way.
    </p>
    <p>
      Negative values are valid and mean "before 1970": −86400 is 31 December 1969. Fractional values are common
      in Python and JavaScript, which store sub-second precision in the decimal part or in a larger unit.
    </p>

    <h2>Seconds, milliseconds, microseconds and nanoseconds</h2>
    <p>
      The same instant can be written with different precision, and the number of digits is the only reliable
      clue. The automatic detection uses it:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Digits</th>
            <th>Unit</th>
            <th>Example</th>
            <th>Typical source</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>10</td>
            <td>Seconds</td>
            <td>1755345600</td>
            <td>Unix tools, PHP, Go, Ruby, JWT exp and iat claims</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>13</td>
            <td>Milliseconds</td>
            <td>1755345600000</td>
            <td>JavaScript Date.now, Java, Kafka, MongoDB</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>16</td>
            <td>Microseconds</td>
            <td>1755345600000000</td>
            <td>Postgres internal timestamps, Chrome traces</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>19</td>
            <td>Nanoseconds</td>
            <td>1755345600000000000</td>
            <td>Go time.UnixNano, Prometheus, InfluxDB</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Detection is a heuristic: a 10-digit value can in principle be milliseconds from 1970. If a converted date
      looks wrong, such as 1970 when you expected last week, or the year 55000, set the unit manually.
    </p>

    <h2>The output formats</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        ISO 8601: the interchange standard, e.g. 2026-08-16T14:05:07+02:00. It sorts correctly as plain text, so
        use it in filenames and log lines. The Z suffix means an offset of zero.
      </li>
      <li>
        RFC 2822 / HTTP date: the email and HTTP header format, e.g. Sat, 16 Aug 2026 12:05:07 GMT. This tool
        always gives it in UTC, as the HTTP specification requires.
      </li>
      <li>
        Relative time: "3 days ago", "in 4 hours", in your browser's locale rules, so it reads naturally in your
        language.
      </li>
      <li>
        Local time: the same instant in the IANA time zone you pick, with the correct daylight-saving offset for
        that date.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Timestamps have no timezone</h2>
    <p>
      This causes many debugging problems. The number 1755345600 does not "mean" 14:00 in Berlin or 08:00 in New
      York. It is one instant, and those are two ways to display it. If a stored timestamp seems several hours
      off, the bug is almost always in the formatting layer or in a local-time parse on input, not in the
      timestamp.
    </p>

    <h2>The year 2038 problem</h2>
    <p>
      A signed 32-bit integer that counts seconds runs out on 19 January 2038 at 03:14:07 UTC, and then wraps to
      December 1901. Modern systems use 64-bit time, and JavaScript numbers safely hold milliseconds up to the
      year 275760, so values after 2038 convert correctly here. The problem remains in embedded firmware, old
      filesystems and legacy C code.
    </p>

    <h2>Leap seconds</h2>
    <p>
      Unix time ignores leap seconds by design: every day is exactly 86,400 seconds. During a leap second the
      same Unix value is used twice, or the clock is smeared over several hours. So a Unix timestamp is not a
      true count of SI seconds since 1970; it is off by the number of leap seconds added since then. The
      arithmetic stays simple and matches what your database reports.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting the current timestamp elsewhere</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Language or tool</th>
            <th>Seconds</th>
            <th>Milliseconds</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Shell</td>
            <td>date +%s</td>
            <td>date +%s%3N</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>JavaScript</td>
            <td>Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000)</td>
            <td>Date.now()</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Python</td>
            <td>int(time.time())</td>
            <td>int(time.time() * 1000)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>PostgreSQL</td>
            <td>SELECT extract(epoch FROM now())::bigint</td>
            <td>multiply by 1000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>MySQL</td>
            <td>SELECT UNIX_TIMESTAMP()</td>
            <td>SELECT UNIX_TIMESTAMP(NOW(3)) * 1000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Go</td>
            <td>time.Now().Unix()</td>
            <td>time.Now().UnixMilli()</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Batch mode</h2>
    <p>
      Paste a column from a log file, a CSV or a query result, one value per line or comma separated. The Unix
      timestamp converter converts up to 2,000 values at once, each with its detected unit, and you can copy the
      table or download it as CSV. It marks each value it cannot read and converts the rest.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
